  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 245 AM EDT Friday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Mainly dry weather, slightly below average \ntemperatures, and lower humidity to end the week and start the \nweekend. \n\nNorthwest flow aloft continues as the low pressure system over the \nNortheast moves away from the area. At the surface, weak high \npressure is settling in. There is still a BKN cloud deck around 7000-\n9000 ft AGL along and east of I-95 in VA...which the models have \nhandled poorly. This cloud deck should gradually scatter out during \nthe rest of the morning. Today will be similar to yesterday with \nlower humidity and highs in the upper 80s, but with light and \nvariable winds. Humidity will likely come up a bit in the evening, \nespecially near the coast as the flow becomes onshore. One last dry \nday (but warmer/more humid) on Saturday as upper heights build a bit \nand the LLVL flow becomes southerly. Highs near 90F w/ aftn dew pts \nrising into the mid-upper 60s.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...A typical summertime pattern returns by late this \nweekend and early next week with increasing shower/storm chances \nstarting Sunday and continuing through early next week.\n\nThe pattern becomes more unsettled late in the weekend into early \nnext week as a slow moving UL trough approaches from the west. At \nthe same time, a weak cold front approaches from the north, but will \nremain north of the local area through Monday (and likely wash out \nby Tue). With the approaching trough and Bermuda high pressure well \noffshore, deep-layered S to SW flow will prevail. This will allow \nrich Gulf moisture to overspread the area on Sunday and remain over \nus early next week. PWs surge from a comfortable 1-1.4\" on Saturday \nto 2.2-2.4\" by late Sunday/Sunday night. Scattered to potentially \nnumerous showers/tstms are expected Sunday. The highest precip \nchances will initially be across south-central VA during the aftn \n(as the deep moisture arrives here first). Showers and storms should \ngradually spread NE through the evening (though it may very well \nremain dry near the immediate coast and on the eastern shore). \nShowers/storms are likely across the entire area on Monday as a lead \nshortwave potentially tracks over the area. Monday appears to be the \nday with the highest convective coverage during the period. The \ntrough dampens a bit from Tue-Wed...but scattered showers and storms \nare still expected during the aftn/evening on both days as weak SW \nflow aloft continues and deep moisture remains nearby. Given the \nenvironment and relatively weak flow aloft, there will be a threat \nof localized flash flooding each day. Temps dip down a little below \naverage during the early week period thanks to the cloud cover and \nmultiple rounds of rain.",
